  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "**This is the easy version of the problem available for 100 points in all divisions. Note that here you need to find the maximum number of items possible.**\n\nThere's a shop with NNN distinct items numbered 111, 222, .... NNN.\nThere's also a boolean array BBB of length NNN.\n\nYou want to buy all NNN items once each, in some order. Say this order is P1,P2,...,PNP_1, P_2, ..., P_NP1​,P2​,...,PN​. When buying item PiP_iPi​,\n\n\n\nIf BPi=1B_{P_i} = 1BPi​​=1 and there exists some j&lt;ij &lt; ij&lt;i such that Pj&gt;PiP_j &gt; P_iPj​&gt;Pi​, you get two copies of item PiP_iPi​, otherwise you get one copy. Informally, you get two copies if you have bought an item with a larger index before, otherwise you get one copy.\n\n\n\n\nIf BPi=0B_{P_i} = 0BPi​​=0, it's the opposite - if there exists some j&lt;ij &lt; ij&lt;i such that Pj&lt;PiP_j &lt; P_iPj​&lt;Pi​, you get two copies, otherwise one. Informally, you get two copies if you have bought an item with a smaller index before, otherwise you get one copy.\n\n\n\nFind the **maximum** total number of items that you can have in the end.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains NNN - the number of items\nThe second line contains NNN boolean variables - B1,B2,...,BNB_1, B_2, ..., B_NB1​,B2​,...,BN​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new the maximum possible number of items.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1041 \\le T \\le 10^41≤T≤104",
      "2≤N≤2⋅1052 \\le N \\le 2 \\cdot 10^52≤N≤2⋅105",
      "Bi=0B_i = 0Bi​=0 or Bi=1B_i = 1Bi​=1",
      "The sum of NNN does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n2\n0 1\n2\n1 1",
        "output": "2\n3",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 1 : It can be proven that regardless of what order we buy the items in, we will always get 111 of each.\nTest Case 2 : Buying the items in order P=[2,1]P = [2, 1]P=[2,1] is optimal, as you get 111 copy of item 222 and 222 copies of item 111."
      }
    ]
